# RISC0 Guest Program Output Standards

## Context
- When implementing RISC0 guest programs that generate formatted output
- When handling proof generation in dev mode vs production
- When working with structured and ASCII art output

## Requirements

### ASCII Art Output
- All lines must be exactly 7 characters wide
- No trailing spaces after lines
- Use consistent line terminators
- Example line formats:
  ```
  ---o---  // changing yang (7 chars)
  --- ---  // unchanging yin (7 chars)
  -------  // unchanging yang (7 chars)
  ---x---  // changing yin (7 chars)
  ```

### Journal Output Structure
- Use consistent marker bytes for different sections
- Structure output in predictable order:
  1. Input digest (32 bytes)
  2. Structured output (with marker)
  3. ASCII art representation
- Log output sizes for verification

### Proof Generation Mode
- Handle dev mode proofs appropriately:
  ```rust
  if cfg!(feature = "dev") {
      warn!("Running in development mode - proofs will not be secure");
  }
  ```
- Expect different receipt types in dev vs production:
  ```rust
  match info.receipt.inner {
      InnerReceipt::Composite(cr) => {
          // Production mode handling
      }
      InnerReceipt::Dev(dr) => {
          // Dev mode handling
      }
      _ => {
          error!("Unexpected receipt type");
          return Err(anyhow::anyhow!("Invalid receipt type"));
      }
  }
  ```

## Examples

### Good Example - ASCII Art Generation
```rust
fn line_to_ascii(line: LineValue) -> String {
    match line {
        LineValue::OldYin => "---x---",    // exactly 7 chars
        LineValue::YoungYin => "--- ---",   // exactly 7 chars
        LineValue::OldYang => "---o---",    // exactly 7 chars
        LineValue::YoungYang => "-------",  // exactly 7 chars
    }.to_string()
}

fn hexagram_to_ascii(hexagram: &HexagramGeneration) -> String {
    let mut ascii_art = String::new();
    for (i, &line) in hexagram.lines.iter().enumerate().rev() {
        ascii_art.push_str(&line_to_ascii(line));
        if i > 0 {
            ascii_art.push('\n');
        }
    }
    ascii_art
}
```

### Bad Example - ASCII Art Generation
```rust
// Don't do this - inconsistent line lengths and spacing
fn line_to_ascii(line: LineValue) -> String {
    match line {
        LineValue::OldYin => "---x--- ",    // extra space
        LineValue::YoungYin => "--- ---",
        LineValue::OldYang => "---o",       // too short
        LineValue::YoungYang => "-------  ", // extra spaces
    }.to_string()
}
```

### Good Example - Receipt Handling
```rust
match info.receipt.inner {
    InnerReceipt::Composite(cr) => {
        info!("Processing composite receipt in production mode");
        let sr = prover.composite_to_succinct(&cr)?;
        process_succinct_receipt(sr)
    }
    InnerReceipt::Dev(dr) => {
        warn!("Processing dev mode receipt - not secure for production");
        process_dev_receipt(dr)
    }
    _ => {
        error!("Unexpected receipt type");
        Err(anyhow::anyhow!("Invalid receipt type"))
    }
}
```

### Bad Example - Receipt Handling
```rust
// Don't do this - doesn't handle different receipt types
if let InnerReceipt::Composite(cr) = info.receipt.inner {
    prover.composite_to_succinct(&cr)?
} else {
    return Err(anyhow::anyhow!("Expected composite receipt"));
}
``` 